// AxISA's NoC mini-SoC (hardware/axisa_core) - a real 3D 8x8x16 mesh
// of XYZW-routed routers (router.v, ported unmodified from rv32i_core),
// each grid position connected to its N/E/S/W/Up/Down neighbors (the W
// axis stays unused at this size - MY_W=0 everywhere, Ana/Kata tied off)
// plus a Local port for whatever AxISA core or memory endpoint sits
// there. TWO independent router networks span the grid - REQUEST
// (core -> memory, FLIT_WIDTH=100) and RESPONSE (memory -> core,
// FLIT_WIDTH=48) - kept as fully separate router instances with zero
// shared state, same as every rv32i_core mesh.
//
// Scaled up MECHANICALLY (no fresh design review - same architecture/
// generator logic already reviewed and live-verified at smaller sizes,
// matching this project's own established scale-up precedent) from the
// prior 4x4x6/96-node mesh to this 8x8x16/1024-node one - by far the
// largest AxISA mesh yet (over 10x the previous size). COORD_BITS bumped
// 3->4 since Z now needs values 0-15. AxISA has only ONE core module
// (cpu_core.v, no pipelined variant), so no P/E split is needed.
//
// Memory lives at (3,3,7) - the lower of two symmetric center
// positions on all three axes (sizes 8/8/16, none of which has an exact
// center this time) - not a corner, same rule every prior mesh in this
// project (both rv32i_core and AxISA) already used.
//
// Grid layout (x,y,z), MEM marked - shown as Z-layer slices:
